    </div>
    <div class="inside-wrap reveal">
      <div class="tab-buttons">
        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="answers">Model Answer Bank</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="company">Company Breakdown</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="coding">Coding Appendix</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="concepts">Concept Sheets</button>
      </div>

      <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-answers">
        <div class="tab-copy">
          <h3>Section 1 · Model Answer Bank</h3>
          <p>The questions that repeat at almost every company in the guide — each with a template for structuring your answer, plus a worked example you adapt with your own details.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>"Tell me about yourself" — a 60-second structure</li>
            <li>"Strengths and weaknesses" — without the clichés</li>
            <li>"Why should we hire you?" / "Why this company?"</li>
            <li>Behavioral (STAR) questions on teamwork and conflict</li>
            <li>Technical concepts likely to get a follow-up (Complete Bank)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mock-page">
          <div class="mock-tag">Section 1 · Sample</div>
          <h5>"Tell me about yourself."</h5>
          <p class="mock-q">Template: present (course, year, college) → one relevant strength or project → why you're here / what you're looking for. Keep it under 60 seconds.</p>
          <div class="mock-a">"I'm a final-year Computer Science student at [college], and I've spent the last year focused on backend development — my strongest project was a [brief description] where I handled [specific part]…"</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-company">
        <div class="tab-copy">
          <h3>Section 2 · Company-by-Company</h3>
          <p>Every recruiter gets its own section: a "how to prepare" note on what that company's rounds actually emphasize, then its HR and technical questions.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>HR round questions specific to that recruiter's style</li>
            <li>Technical questions grouped by subject (OOP, DBMS, OS, C/C++…)</li>
            <li>What that company's interviewers tend to weight most</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mock-page">
          <div class="mock-tag">Part A · Company 1 of 23</div>
          <h5>TCS — NQT-track roles (Ninja / Digital / Prime)</h5>
          <p class="mock-q" style="font-style:normal; color:var(--text-dim); font-size:.82rem;">TCS interviewers often open with a casual, off-script line to see how you handle being thrown off — answer briefly and naturally, then move to your prepared material.</p>
          <ul class="mock-list">
            <li>· What's your USP? Are you okay with relocation?</li>
            <li>· Java vs. C# vs. C — key differences</li>
            <li>· Write a SQL query using LIKE vs. =</li>
            <li>· Explain any project on your resume, end-to-end</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-coding">
        <div class="tab-copy">
          <h3>Section 3 · Coding Solutions Appendix <em style="color:var(--text-faint); font-style:normal; font-size:.8rem;">(Complete Bank only)</em></h3>
          <p>The recurring "write a program to…" questions, worked end-to-end — not just described, actually solved.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>6 fully worked programs covering the patterns that show up across service-company technical rounds</li>
            <li>Clean, commented code you can walk an interviewer through line by line</li>
            <li>Picked for frequency, not novelty — the ones that actually get asked</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mock-page">
          <div class="mock-tag">Section 3 · Sample entry</div>
          <h5>"Write a C program for the Fibonacci series."</h5>
          <div class="mock-a">Iterative solution walkthrough, expected output, and the follow-up interviewers usually ask next — how you'd adapt it to a recursive version.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-concepts">
        <div class="tab-copy">
          <h3>Section 4 · Concept Quick-Reference Sheets <em style="color:var(--text-faint); font-style:normal; font-size:.8rem;">(Complete Bank only)</em></h3>
          <p>9 topics condensed to a page each, so you can close a knowledge gap instead of just seeing that it exists.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>OOP fundamentals, DBMS &amp; normalization, OS scheduling</li>
            <li>Testing types, SDLC models, data structure basics</li>
            <li>C/C++ memory model, networking &amp; cloud fundamentals</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mock-page">
          <div class="mock-tag">Section 4 · Sample sheet</div>
          <h5>OOP — the four pillars</h5>
          <div class="mock-a">Encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism — one paragraph each, worded the way you'd actually say it out loud in an interview.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

